From baa7ee52aaf992b799ac162b1238acac58a31c12 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TickTrade Workbench <hmodebdwie1@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 19:47:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] fix(creator): DataBank honesty + ranking cost, and a
 CPU-aware script supervisor

Three defects found by running the stack, not by reading it.

1. Six DataBank fields shipped as a bare null with no explanation.
   The 10 benchmark.* percentiles are not in the catalog's dependency map, so
   hydrate() could never derive a reason for them; when their source metric was
   not finite they rendered as `"value": null, "reason": ""`. The bank contract
   says an unavailable field keeps its definition AND states why. hydrate() now
   gives any field that resolves to null an explicit reason and marks it
   unavailable rather than presenting it as an observation.
   Measured over 4 rounds x 8 profiles: 200 reasons added, 200 sources corrected
   from observed-window to unavailable, and ZERO computed values changed.
   Unavailable fields carrying a reason went from 84/90 to 90/90.

2. Percentile ranking was O(records^2 x specs).
   Each record rebuilt one value array per rank spec and percentileRank sorted it
   again - 400 records x 8 specs = 3200 sorts of a 400-element array per build.
   Cohorts and rank ladders are now built once and ranked by binary search on the
   pre-sorted ladder, using the same below + equal/2 formula. Percentiles are
   bit-identical (verified by the same before/after diff as above).

3. A healthy script could be killed by the supervisor's wall-clock deadline.
   The guest charges its own 80ms budget in CPU time, so Docker throttling cannot
   fail a script there - but the parent's 8s deadline was pure wall clock and
   SIGKILLed the shared worker, discarding every other script queued on that pool.
   Reproduced deterministically: with 6 spinners on 4 CPUs the runtime suite went
   24/25, failing "memory growth is stopped inside the guest" at exactly 8008ms
   with "Script exceeded its execution deadline".
   Measured cause: exhausting the 16MiB guest limit needs ~7s of CPU (6.35s wall
   at 112% of a core when idle), so the 8s backstop had almost no margin and any
   contention pre-empted the inner limit.
   The deadline now reads the child's real CPU from /proc/<pid>/stat and grants at
   most 2 extra periods when the child got less than 0.9 of a core - i.e. only
   when the box starved it. A script that burns CPU never qualifies and its
   ceiling is unchanged. Linux only; elsewhere the behaviour is exactly as before.
   Evidence: under saturation one extension is granted and the bomb then stops on
   the correct inner limit ("out of memory", ok:false) at 11.1s instead of being
   SIGKILLed. A pure CPU spin on an idle box is still caught by the guest's 80ms
   interrupt in 634ms and never reaches the supervisor at all.
   Worst case on a contended box rises from 8s to 24s for a script that escapes
   the guest CPU interrupt through native allocation/GC work.

Suites: tickscript 25/25 unloaded AND 25/25 under CPU saturation (was 24/25),
api jest 57 suites / 319 tests.
